1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,782
Water in a glass.

2
00:00:01,782 --> 00:00:03,164
Sugar in your tea.

3
00:00:03,164 --> 00:00:05,560
Cooking gas in the kitchen cylinder.

4
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:07,573
None of these is made of ions.

5
00:00:07,573 --> 00:00:09,595
Their atoms hold on by sharing electrons.

6
00:00:10,045 --> 00:00:14,148
Diya notices that sugar melts
easily in a pan, but salt does not.

7
00:00:14,148 --> 00:00:15,347
Why so different?

8
00:00:15,697 --> 00:00:17,200
Salt is built from ions.

9
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,636
Sugar is built from molecules,
held together by shared pairs.

10
00:00:21,136 --> 00:00:25,804
By the end, you will draw the Lewis
structure of any simple molecule, step

11
00:00:25,804 --> 00:00:26,704
by step.

12
00:00:27,204 --> 00:00:28,418
First, a picture.

13
00:00:28,418 --> 00:00:31,507
Two kids each own one toy
car, and each wants two.

14
00:00:31,507 --> 00:00:34,226
So they agree to play
with both cars together.

15
00:00:34,576 --> 00:00:36,609
Atoms do the same with electrons.

16
00:00:36,609 --> 00:00:40,692
Two atoms each put one electron into
a shared pair, and both count that

17
00:00:40,692 --> 00:00:41,592
pair.

18
00:00:41,595 --> 00:00:44,456
Lewis and Langmuir called
this a covalent bond.

19
00:00:44,456 --> 00:00:47,949
One shared pair makes a single
bond, drawn as one line.

20
00:00:48,349 --> 00:00:50,774
Two shared pairs make a double bond.

21
00:00:50,774 --> 00:00:52,541
Three make a triple bond.

22
00:00:52,941 --> 00:00:55,854
Pairs that are not shared
are called lone pairs.

23
00:00:55,854 --> 00:00:57,361
They stay on one atom.

24
00:00:58,011 --> 00:01:00,659
Let's build some on our molecule bench.

25
00:01:00,659 --> 00:01:02,607
First, two chlorine atoms.

26
00:01:02,607 --> 00:01:04,143
Each has seven dots.

27
00:01:04,493 --> 00:01:07,053
Each moves one electron into the middle.

28
00:01:07,053 --> 00:01:09,220
One shared pair, a single bond.

29
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:11,233
Each chlorine now counts eight.

30
00:01:11,633 --> 00:01:12,553
Now water.

31
00:01:12,553 --> 00:01:14,127
Oxygen has six dots.

32
00:01:14,127 --> 00:01:15,808
Each hydrogen has one.

33
00:01:16,208 --> 00:01:18,939
Oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen.

34
00:01:18,939 --> 00:01:20,349
Oxygen counts eight.

35
00:01:20,349 --> 00:01:23,740
Each hydrogen counts two,
the full shell of helium.

36
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,349
Now oxygen gas.

37
00:01:25,349 --> 00:01:27,294
Each oxygen needs two more.

38
00:01:27,294 --> 00:01:29,086
So they share two pairs.

39
00:01:29,086 --> 00:01:30,243
A double bond.

40
00:01:30,643 --> 00:01:31,865
And nitrogen gas.

41
00:01:31,865 --> 00:01:33,869
Each nitrogen needs three more.

42
00:01:33,869 --> 00:01:35,186
Three shared pairs.

43
00:01:35,186 --> 00:01:36,264
A triple bond.

44
00:01:36,914 --> 00:01:37,869
Side by side.

45
00:01:37,869 --> 00:01:40,681
Ionic: electrons move
from one atom to another.

46
00:01:40,681 --> 00:01:43,299
Covalent: electrons are
shared between atoms.

47
00:01:43,699 --> 00:01:44,810
Pause and think.

48
00:01:44,810 --> 00:01:48,746
In water, does oxygen take the
hydrogen electrons, or share them?

49
00:01:51,946 --> 00:01:52,846
It shares!

50
00:01:52,778 --> 00:01:56,659
Oxygen and hydrogen are both
non metals, so they share pairs.

51
00:01:57,309 --> 00:01:59,797
Now the recipe for any Lewis structure.

52
00:02:00,147 --> 00:02:01,047
Step one.

53
00:02:00,882 --> 00:02:03,652
Add up the valence
electrons of all the atoms.

54
00:02:03,652 --> 00:02:06,144
For a negative ion, add one per charge.

55
00:02:06,144 --> 00:02:08,359
For a positive ion, take one away.

56
00:02:09,109 --> 00:02:10,009
Step two.

57
00:02:09,899 --> 00:02:11,181
Draw the skeleton.

58
00:02:11,181 --> 00:02:14,739
The least electronegative atom
usually goes in the middle.

59
00:02:14,739 --> 00:02:17,216
Hydrogen and fluorine sit at the ends.

60
00:02:17,966 --> 00:02:18,866
Step three.

61
00:02:18,757 --> 00:02:20,701
Join the atoms with single bonds.

62
00:02:20,701 --> 00:02:22,421
Each bond uses two electrons.

63
00:02:23,171 --> 00:02:24,071
Step four.

64
00:02:24,035 --> 00:02:27,151
Give the rest as lone
pairs to complete octets.

65
00:02:27,151 --> 00:02:31,856
If an atom is still short, turn lone
pairs into double or triple bonds.

66
00:02:32,606 --> 00:02:33,580
Remember this.

67
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:35,293
Count, connect, complete.

68
00:02:35,293 --> 00:02:36,787
Then check every octet.

69
00:02:37,487 --> 00:02:39,447
Now, one worked example.

70
00:02:39,447 --> 00:02:40,674
Carbon dioxide.

71
00:02:40,974 --> 00:02:41,874
Count.

72
00:02:41,525 --> 00:02:44,577
Carbon has four valence
electrons, each oxygen six.

73
00:02:44,577 --> 00:02:46,337
Four plus twelve is sixteen.

74
00:02:46,837 --> 00:02:47,737
Connect.

75
00:02:47,528 --> 00:02:52,081
Carbon is less electronegative, so it
sits in the middle, with two single

76
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:52,981
bonds.

77
00:02:52,674 --> 00:02:53,822
That uses four.

78
00:02:53,822 --> 00:02:55,018
Twelve are left.

79
00:02:55,768 --> 00:02:56,668
Complete.

80
00:02:56,426 --> 00:02:57,938
Give six to each oxygen.

81
00:02:57,938 --> 00:03:00,739
But now carbon has only
four electrons around it.

82
00:03:01,489 --> 00:03:05,939
So each oxygen turns one lone pair
into a shared pair with carbon.

83
00:03:06,689 --> 00:03:09,838
Now we have O double bond C double bond O.

84
00:03:09,838 --> 00:03:13,455
Every atom has eight, and
we used exactly sixteen.

85
00:03:13,455 --> 00:03:14,355
Done.

86
00:03:14,703 --> 00:03:17,826
Boards like ISC also ask
for the coordinate bond.

87
00:03:17,826 --> 00:03:21,545
Here both electrons of the shared
pair come from the same atom.

88
00:03:21,945 --> 00:03:24,183
Ammonia has a lone pair on nitrogen.

89
00:03:24,183 --> 00:03:26,611
A hydrogen ion has no electrons at all.

90
00:03:27,011 --> 00:03:31,551
Nitrogen gives its whole pair to the
hydrogen ion, and we get the ammonium

91
00:03:31,551 --> 00:03:32,451
ion.

92
00:03:32,045 --> 00:03:35,046
We draw this bond as an
arrow from the giver.

93
00:03:35,696 --> 00:03:37,157
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:03:37,507 --> 00:03:41,747
Students count only the bonds and forget
the lone pairs, so the total comes

95
00:03:41,747 --> 00:03:42,647
out wrong.

96
00:03:42,926 --> 00:03:47,353
In water, two bonds use four electrons,
and two lone pairs use four more.

97
00:03:47,353 --> 00:03:50,167
Eight in all, matching
one plus one plus six.

98
00:03:50,817 --> 00:03:52,177
Where is this asked?

99
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:56,357
Expect questions that ask you to draw
Lewis structures, and to count bond

100
00:03:56,357 --> 00:03:57,763
pairs and lone pairs.

101
00:03:58,363 --> 00:03:59,498
Exam level now.

102
00:03:59,498 --> 00:04:03,854
In the Lewis structure of NF three,
find the total valence electrons,

103
00:04:03,854 --> 00:04:05,932
and the lone pairs on nitrogen.

104
00:04:05,932 --> 00:04:07,115
Pause and solve.

105
00:04:12,515 --> 00:04:13,653
The answer is A.

106
00:04:13,653 --> 00:04:16,731
Twenty six electrons, and
one lone pair on nitrogen.

107
00:04:17,231 --> 00:04:20,891
Five for nitrogen, plus three
times seven for fluorine.

108
00:04:20,891 --> 00:04:21,816
Twenty six.

109
00:04:22,316 --> 00:04:23,832
Three bonds use six.

110
00:04:23,832 --> 00:04:27,321
Each fluorine takes three
lone pairs, eighteen more.

111
00:04:27,821 --> 00:04:32,442
Twenty six minus twenty four leaves two
electrons: one lone pair on nitrogen.

112
00:04:33,042 --> 00:04:34,582
Here is your recap.

113
00:04:34,582 --> 00:04:36,164
Screenshot this card.

114
00:04:39,364 --> 00:04:41,550
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:04:41,950 --> 00:04:42,850
One.

116
00:04:42,379 --> 00:04:45,671
What is the difference between
a lone pair and a bond pair?

117
00:04:46,471 --> 00:04:47,371
Two.

118
00:04:46,882 --> 00:04:50,024
How many valence electrons does
carbon dioxide have in total?

119
00:04:50,824 --> 00:04:51,724
Three.

120
00:04:51,322 --> 00:04:54,258
How many shared pairs are
there in a nitrogen molecule?

121
00:04:55,158 --> 00:04:58,297
Next time: two Lewis
structures can both look right.

122
00:04:58,297 --> 00:05:00,294
How do you pick the better one?

123
00:05:00,294 --> 00:05:02,147
We find out in formal charge.
